From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Eggleston <eggnet@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:36:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Take the alert of a RtlWaitOnAddress() wake that races
 a timeout.

RtlWakeAddressSingle() and RtlWakeAddressAll() dequeue a waiter under
the queue lock and alert it after releasing the lock. A waiter whose
wait times out in between finds itself dequeued but returns
STATUS_TIMEOUT. Its alert then arrives with nobody waiting for it and
stays pending on the thread, and the thread's next
NtWaitForAlertByThreadId() returns at once whatever it waits for:
typically a SleepConditionVariableCS() or SleepConditionVariableSRW()
with an infinite timeout on an unrelated condition variable, which
wakes up spuriously. The comment in RtlWakeAddressAll() accepts that
as the price of not alerting under the spinlock.

Windows leaves no alert pending after such a race: a waiter that a
waker has claimed takes the wake before returning. Do the same: a
waiter that finds itself dequeued when its wait timed out has been
woken, so take the in-flight alert with an untimed
NtWaitForAlertByThreadId() and report the wake. The wakers still alert
outside the lock, and the second wait lasts only until the waker's
alert, which follows its unlock. Update the comment.

Zero-timeout waits make the race common: they are queued like any
other wait and time out immediately, and spinlock back-offs that poll
with WaitOnAddress(..., 0), such as tcmalloc's, collect the stale
alert. Warhammer 40,000: Space Marine 2 crashes because of this when
an unlooped SleepConditionVariableCS(INFINITE) returns while file
reads are still pending.

Wine-Bug: https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=60523
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dlls/ntdll/sync.c | 11 ++++++++---
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
index 729d7cbd847..cc05308c715 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
@@ -883,6 +883,7 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI RtlWaitOnAddress( const void *addr, const void *cmp, SIZE_T size
 {
     struct futex_queue *queue = get_futex_queue( addr );
     struct futex_entry entry;
+    BOOL queued = FALSE;
     NTSTATUS ret;
 
     TRACE("addr %p cmp %p size %#Ix timeout %s\n", addr, cmp, size, debugstr_timeout( timeout ));
@@ -915,11 +916,15 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI RtlWaitOnAddress( const void *addr, const void *cmp, SIZE_T size
     if (ReadPointerAcquire( (void * volatile *)&entry.addr ))
     {
         spin_lock( &queue->lock );
-        if (entry.addr)
+        if ((queued = !!entry.addr))
             list_remove( &entry.entry );
         spin_unlock( &queue->lock );
     }
 
+    /* A waker took us off the queue and alerts us after releasing the lock. If our wait timed out first, take
+     * that alert, or it would end this thread's next wait early. */
+    if (!queued && ret == STATUS_TIMEOUT) ret = NtWaitForAlertByThreadId( NULL, NULL );
+
     TRACE("returning %#lx\n", ret);
 
     if (ret == STATUS_ALERTED) ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
@@ -962,8 +967,8 @@ void WINAPI RtlWakeAddressAll( const void *addr )
         }
     }
 
-    /* Try not to make a system call while holding a spinlock (even if that can be responsible for spurious wake
-     * up scenario). */
+    /* Try not to make a system call while holding a spinlock. A waiter whose wait times out before the alert
+     * arrives takes it in RtlWaitOnAddress(). */
     spin_unlock( &queue->lock );
     if (count)
         NtAlertMultipleThreadByThreadId( tids, count, NULL, NULL );
